course: biology
lesson: 65
title: The heart and blood
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Put your hand flat on your chest and you feel it: thump, thump, your heart, a muscle about the size of your fist that never stops working from before you are born.
explain: Each beat squeezes blood out through tubes called arteries to every part of you, then the blood comes back through veins, round and round in a loop, carrying oxygen and food and taking waste away.
explain: Through a doctor's stethoscope the beat sounds like «lub-dub»: that is not the muscle squeezing but little doors, the valves, snapping shut so the blood can only go one way.
explain: Blood is always red, bright red with oxygen and dark red without, even though veins look blue through your skin.
explain: The heart grows stronger with exercise like any muscle, and it looks nothing like the heart on a Valentine's card.
fact: The heart is a muscle about the size of your fist, in the middle of the chest tipped a little to the left, and it beats about 100,000 times a day.
fact: The lub-dub sound is the heart's valves snapping shut: «lub» when the valves between the upper and lower chambers close, «dub» when the valves at the exits close.
fact: The pulse is the stretch of an artery each time the heart pumps; you can feel it on the wrist or neck, and it gets faster when you run, because working muscles need more oxygen; a fright makes it faster too, as the body gets ready to run.
fact: Blood is a yellowish liquid called plasma carrying red cells, white cells and platelets; a drop the size of a pinhead holds about 5 million red blood cells.
fact: Red blood cells carry oxygen using iron-rich haemoglobin, white cells fight germs, and platelets help stop bleeding.
fact: Blood is always red: bright red with oxygen and darker red after giving it away; veins only look blue because of how light passes through skin.
fact: The heart pumps blood out through arteries, into tiny capillaries and back through veins; its right side pumps blood to the lungs and its left side pumps it to the rest of the body.
fact: In 1628 William Harvey showed that the same blood goes round and round the body, when many people still believed blood was made fresh in the liver and used up; but the heart pumps more blood in an hour than food could ever make.
fact: When you cut yourself, platelets stick together at the cut and threads of a protein called fibrin form a net that traps blood cells into a clot, which dries into a scab.
fact: An adult has about 5 litres of blood, and healthy adults can give about half a litre at a blood donation to help people after accidents, operations and some illnesses.
fact: A giraffe's heart weighs about 11 kg and pumps at about twice the pressure of ours to push blood some 2 metres up its neck to the brain, and special blood vessels in its neck stop it fainting when it lifts its head after drinking.
fact: The heart gets stronger with exercise and then pumps more blood with each beat, so fit people often have a slower heartbeat at rest.
fact: A real heart is shaped more like a rounded cone with big tubes coming out of the top; the Valentine heart is only a drawing.
exercise: About how big is your heart? | About the size of your fist | About the size of a football | About the size of a small grape | About the size of your whole head
exercise: What kind of thing is the heart? | A muscle that pumps blood | A bag that stores food | A bone that protects the lungs | A sponge that cleans the air
exercise: About how many times does the heart beat in a day? | About 100,000 | About 100 in all | About 1,000 or so | About 10 million
exercise: What makes the lub-dub sound in a stethoscope? | The heart's valves snapping shut | Blood splashing about in the stomach | The lungs clapping together | The ribs knocking on the heart
exercise: What is the pulse you feel on your wrist? | An artery stretching each time the heart pumps | A small muscle in the wrist twitching by itself | Air moving in your arm | A nerve sending a message
exercise: Why is your pulse faster after running? | Your muscles need more oxygen, so the heart pumps faster | Your blood gets too hot from running and starts to boil | Your heart is scared of running | Your wrist was squashed while running
exercise: What is the liquid part of blood called? | Plasma | Platelets | Bone marrow | Saliva, or spit
exercise: What do red blood cells do? | Carry oxygen around the body | Fight the germs that get in through cuts | Make bones grow longer | Digest food in the stomach
exercise: What colour is the blood inside your veins? | Dark red | Bright blue | Dark green | Purple-black
exercise: Why do veins look blue through your skin? | Because of the way light passes through skin | Because the blood in them is blue until it meets air | Because veins are painted blue inside | Because there is no blood in them
exercise: Which way does blood go round the body? | Out through arteries, through capillaries and back through veins | Out through the veins to the toes, and then it never comes back again | Only up and down inside the legs | Round the stomach and then out of the skin
exercise: Where does the right side of the heart pump blood? | To the lungs | To the brain first | To the tips of the feet | Into the stomach
exercise: What did William Harvey show in 1628? | The same blood goes round and round the body | Blood is made fresh every day in the liver from the food we eat | The heart is the place where we think | Blood is blue inside the body
exercise: What stops a small cut from bleeding? | Platelets and fibrin threads form a clot | Air turns the blood into stone | The skin sucks the blood back in through its tiny holes | White cells eat the blood
exercise: About how much blood does a healthy adult give at a blood donation? | About half a litre | About 5 litres | About a teaspoon | About 2 litres, a big bottle
exercise: Why does a giraffe need a very strong heart? | To push blood about 2 metres up its neck to the brain | To help it run faster than a cheetah when lions chase it | To keep its long legs warm | To digest tough leaves
exercise: What happens to the heart when you exercise often? | It gets stronger and pumps more blood with each beat | It gets smaller and weaker | It stops needing to beat at night while you are fast asleep | It moves to the right side
exercise: What shape is a real heart? | More like a rounded cone with tubes at the top | Exactly like the hearts on a Valentine's card | A perfect round ball | A long thin tube
test: bro, the blood in ur veins is blue till it hits air, everyone knows that | No, blood is always red, just darker red without oxygen | Yes, it turns red in the air the second you cut yourself, bro | Yes, but only in your arms | No, it is green inside the body
test: In a cartoon the hero's heart jumps out of his chest shaped like a Valentine card. Put it right. | A real heart is a cone-shaped muscle with tubes, inside the ribs | Real hearts are exactly that shape | Real hearts can jump out a little way when you are very much in love | Real hearts are square
test: What would happen if a valve in the heart did not close properly? | Some blood would leak backwards and the heart would work harder | The heart would pump twice as well | The blood would turn blue | Nothing at all, valves are not really needed, they are just spare parts
test: Before Harvey, people thought the liver made new blood all day. Why could that not be true? | The heart pumps more blood in an hour than food could ever make | The liver is far too small to hold any of the body's blood | The liver is made of bone | Blood never moves at all
test: Kai cuts his finger and it stops bleeding in a few minutes. What did his body do? | Platelets and fibrin made a clot that plugged the cut | His heart stopped beating for a few minutes | His blood ran out | The cold air froze the blood solid
test: My uncle's a marathon runner and his heart beats slowly when he rests. is that bad?? | No, a fit heart pumps more blood each beat, so it needs fewer | Yes, his heart is getting tired and stopping | Yes, it means he has too little blood | No, it means his heart has stopped growing and has reached its full size
test: shut up and count it: 20 beats in 15 seconds, what's that a minute | 80 beats a minute | 20 beats a minute | 35 beats a minute | 60 beats a minute
test: Why does a giraffe not faint when it lifts its head after drinking? | Its strong heart and special blood vessels keep blood flowing to its brain | It always drinks lying down | Its brain is in its neck | It holds its breath to keep blood in its head
test: A van at the shopping centre says «Give blood, save lives». Who might need the blood? | People after accidents and operations | People who are thirsty | Only vampires in films | People who want to grow taller and stronger
test: In a video game your health bar refills when red hearts float into you. What really refills lost blood? | The bone marrow slowly makes new blood cells | Eating red sweets | Drinking ketchup or tomato juice | Hearts floating in the air
test: Why does your heart beat faster when you get a fright, even standing still? | The body gets ready to run, so the heart sends more blood to muscles | Fear makes the blood boil | The heart is trying to escape | Fright makes the blood turn blue
exam: Identify the origin of the sounds perceived through a stethoscope placed upon the chest. | The closure of the valves of the heart | The contraction of the lungs | The flow of blood within the veins of the arm | The movement of the ribs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the colour of human blood within the veins? | It is dark red, its apparent blueness arising from the optics of the skin | It is blue until exposed to air | It is colourless until oxygenated | It is green owing to its iron content
exam: Ascertain the principal finding published by William Harvey in the year 1628. | Blood circulates continuously around the body | Blood is manufactured afresh in the liver | The heart is the seat of thought and of the passions | The arteries contain only air
exam: It is given that a small cut ceases to bleed after several minutes. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Platelets and fibrin form a clot that seals the wound | The blood is reabsorbed by the skin | The heart ceases to pump temporarily | The air solidifies the blood
